 :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1d9fcb2-0e92-5f35-b647-4cfcb2008919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548672} {:answer/id #uuid "b539aad4-5e88-5ef3-878d-5fd523df2373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548717} {:answer/id #uuid "d69fb37a-d886-507e-aa1b-51da676feb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548772} {:answer/id #uuid "f903c13f-2600-543d-9e79-c26839361f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548672} :answer-field/id #uuid "da4b59a2-41bc-57f1-ab7b-fd67deaeecb8" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548964} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165b289f-d693-54fe-af97-1dce7f847eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548394} {:answer/id #uuid "21ec05be-e542-5b06-82da-b8b77d9acb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548420} {:answer/id #uuid "622f4f53-fa0e-5197-82b7-1833e8bc7e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548543} {:answer/id #uuid "bf97f895-53fa-56ee-9bbf-f47253359595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548543} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2e3317-5784-5bcd-a80a-10ea98305234" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548966} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2e59d6-51a7-5fe2-89d0-05e8293c8d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548499} {:answer/id #uuid "52c6937e-fe66-516e-a4b1-fac71d99f3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548510} {:answer/id #uuid "71ccef85-acdf-5822-b7cd-1c90700e66a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548566} {:answer/id #uuid "cecfcc4e-2ba5-5f52-b0ef-793f85fe11dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548510} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a85cef-fa7c-5a3f-95d7-68572b164e28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "84533a55-9193-5a76-a277-23539897c843" :question/math-academy-id "q-363663" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $5$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-4)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbc82354-7dd4-5242-8b45-d0398f57ee39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223c8e67-5171-5ea9-813f-ad2c34f62661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496728} {:answer/id #uuid "78c5338f-e189-5564-b2bd-1367f0bf0775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496912} {:answer/id #uuid "b22038de-df21-574b-a2d3-9931b67c69bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497022} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e9bd21-b777-5f10-97d3-56334fae5657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497115} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b4cead-3dc7-5c6a-98ef-f3fe49303b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496912} :answer-field/id #uuid "f80968a1-b5d3-5a74-a9ab-07fd72693189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c32a996-4944-5e2d-afe2-519907ae493e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140962" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eef19d66-db46-502e-88ca-61fb86cd6bca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554733}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553861}]}
